  "problemStatement": {
    "formal": "\\text{For a polygonal curve of total length } L \\text{ dropped on parallel planes with spacing } d,\\; \\mathbb{E}[\\mathrm{crossings}] = \\frac{L}{2d}.\\; \\text{Equivalently, } \\alpha_3 = \\mathbb{E}_{S^2}[|\\cos \\varphi|] = \\frac{1}{2} < \\frac{2}{\\pi} = \\alpha_2.",
    "plain": "Proves the 3D Buffon formula: a polygonal curve of total length L dropped on a family of parallel planes (spacing d) has expected crossing count E[crossings] = L/(2d). The key is the sphere average E_{S²}[|cos φ|] = 1/2, proved by computing ∫₀^π sin θ |cos θ| dθ = 1 via the substitution |cos θ| = cos θ on [0,π/2] and |cos θ| = -cos θ on [π/2,π]. Compares with the 2D Buffon formula 2L/(πd), proving the 3D crossing factor α₃ = 1/2 is strictly less than the 2D factor α₂ = 2/π.",
    "whyMatters": [
      "Connects the classical Buffon needle probability calculation to polygonal curves in three dimensions.",
      "Isolates the dimensional constant as a sphere average, reducing the 3D crossing factor to an explicit integral.",
      "Records the comparison between the 3D factor 1/2 and the 2D Buffon factor 2/pi."
    ]
  },
